            'title' => 'Defining and Differentiating Child Language Disorders',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Developmental language disorder (DLD) affects approximately 7% of children and involves persistent difficulty acquiring and using language in the absence of a known biomedical cause such as hearing loss, autism, or intellectual disability. When a language impairment co-occurs with another condition, clinicians use the term "language disorder associated with X" rather than DLD, following the CATALISE consensus terminology published by Bishop and colleagues.</p><p>Core features of DLD include reduced mean length of utterance, difficulty with grammatical morphemes (verb tense marking, plurals), limited vocabulary breadth and depth, and weak narrative macrostructure. These difficulties persist beyond the normal range of variation seen in typically developing peers and are not explained by insufficient exposure to the language being assessed.</p><p>Early identification matters because untreated language disorder is strongly associated with later literacy difficulty, social-emotional problems, and reduced academic achievement. Clinicians must therefore distinguish true disorder from normal variation, bilingual language development, and dialectal difference before assigning a diagnosis.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A bilingual four-year-old is referred for "unclear speech and small vocabulary" by a preschool teacher who only speaks English. What steps would you take before concluding this child has a language disorder rather than typical bilingual development, and how would you communicate your reasoning to the teacher?']],
