        [
            'title' => 'Comprehensive Language Assessment',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A comprehensive language assessment combines standardized norm-referenced testing with naturalistic language sampling. Tools such as the Clinical Evaluation of Language Fundamentals (CELF-5) and the Preschool Language Scale (PLS-5) provide standard scores for comparison to same-age peers, but they sample language in a constrained, decontextualized way and can miss functional communication strengths and weaknesses.</p><p>Language sample analysis, typically using a 50-100 utterance conversational or narrative sample transcribed and analyzed for mean length of utterance, type-token ratio, and grammatical accuracy, captures how a child actually uses language in connected speech. Narrative assessment tools, such as retelling a wordless picture book, reveal macrostructure skills (story grammar elements) and microstructure skills (cohesive ties, complex syntax) that predict later academic language success.</p><p>Dynamic assessment, in which the clinician provides graduated prompting and measures modifiability, is particularly valuable for distinguishing disorder from difference in bilingual or dialectally diverse children, since it measures learning potential rather than prior exposure to a specific linguistic code.</p>']],
