                    ['question' => 'What is the primary advantage of dynamic assessment with bilingual children?', 'options' => [['text' => 'It measures learning potential (modifiability) rather than prior exposure to the test language', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'It is quicker than static testing', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It does not require an interpreter', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It produces a standard score', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Dynamic assessment uses graduated prompting to measure how much a child can learn in the moment, which helps separate disorder from difference caused by limited exposure.'],
                    ['question' => 'What does mean length of utterance (MLU) primarily index?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Grammatical/morphosyntactic development reflected in utterance length', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Articulation accuracy', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Vocabulary diversity only', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Pragmatic appropriateness', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'MLU, calculated in morphemes per utterance, is a well-established index of grammatical complexity and development in young children.'],
