        [
            'title' => 'Evidence-Based Intervention Approaches',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Intervention approaches for child language disorders are typically selected according to the child\'s age, severity, and specific profile. Milieu teaching and enhanced milieu teaching embed language targets within naturally occurring play and routines, using strategies such as time delay, modeling, and mand-model to prompt communication in motivating contexts, and are well supported for toddlers and preschoolers.</p><p>Focused stimulation involves providing a high frequency of models of a specific target form (for example, past tense -ed) within meaningful activities without requiring the child to produce it, relying on repeated, salient input to drive acquisition. For school-age children, narrative intervention explicitly teaches story grammar elements (character, setting, problem, action, resolution) and complex syntax, which supports both oral language and later reading comprehension.</p><p>Across approaches, dosage (frequency and intensity of intervention), generalization planning, and caregiver coaching are critical to outcomes. Clinicians increasingly train parents and teachers to deliver language facilitation strategies throughout the day rather than relying solely on clinician-delivered sessions.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'case_study', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Amir is a 5-year-old referred for an individualized education program evaluation. His MLU is 3.8 morphemes (expected: 5.5+), he omits third-person singular -s and past tense -ed consistently, and his narrative retell lacks a clear problem/resolution structure. Nonverbal cognitive testing is within normal limits, and hearing screening passed. His parents report similar early language difficulties in an older sibling. Based on this profile, the team is considering narrative intervention combined with focused stimulation on verb morphology, delivered through a push-in classroom model with parent coaching for home carryover.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
