        [
            'title' => 'Progress Monitoring and Family Collaboration',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'research_reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Ongoing progress monitoring distinguishes effective intervention from stagnant service delivery. Clinicians should set measurable, functional goals (for example, "will produce regular past tense -ed in 80% of obligatory contexts across three consecutive probes") and track progress using curriculum-based or criterion-referenced probes rather than relying solely on annual standardized re-testing, which is too infrequent and insensitive to short-term change.</p><p>Family collaboration is central to generalization. Caregivers who are coached in strategies such as expansion, extension, and open-ended questioning during daily routines produce measurable gains in their child\'s expressive language, and coaching models (clinician models, caregiver practices with feedback) are more effective than simply handing out a handout of strategies.</p><p>Discharge or goal-adjustment decisions should be data-driven, considering not only standard scores but also functional impact on classroom participation, peer interaction, and literacy readiness.</p>']],
