        [
            'title' => 'Neuroanatomy and Classification of Aphasia',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Aphasia is an acquired language disorder resulting from focal brain damage, most commonly stroke affecting the territory of the left middle cerebral artery in right-handed individuals. Broca\'s aphasia results from damage to the posterior inferior frontal gyrus and produces nonfluent, effortful, agrammatic speech with relatively preserved comprehension of everyday conversation, though comprehension of complex syntax is often impaired.</p><p>Wernicke\'s aphasia results from damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus and produces fluent, grammatically intact-sounding but often meaningless or paraphasic speech, with significantly impaired auditory comprehension. Conduction aphasia, associated with damage to the arcuate fasciculus connecting these regions, features fluent speech with disproportionately impaired repetition and frequent phonemic paraphasias.</p><p>Global aphasia involves severe impairment across all language modalities and typically follows extensive damage to the perisylvian language zone. Transcortical and anomic aphasias round out the classical Boston classification, which organizes subtypes along the dimensions of fluency, auditory comprehension, and repetition ability.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A client presents with fluent, grammatically well-formed speech that is nearly empty of meaningful content, intact repetition, but poor auditory comprehension. Walk through your clinical reasoning for narrowing this to a specific aphasia subtype, and name at least one other condition that could initially mimic this presentation.']],
