            'title' => 'Standardized Aphasia Assessment',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>The Western Aphasia Battery-Revised (WAB-R) is widely used because it yields an Aphasia Quotient summarizing severity and classifies clients into a specific aphasia subtype based on performance across spontaneous speech, auditory comprehension, repetition, and naming subtests. The Boston Diagnostic Aphasia Examination (BDAE) offers a more granular profile across reading, writing, and praxis, useful for detailed treatment planning and research.</p><p>Beyond battery scores, functional communication measures such as the Communicative Effectiveness Index capture how well a client communicates in daily life, which does not always correlate tightly with standardized test scores. Discourse analysis, examining connected speech for informativeness and efficiency, adds further ecological validity.</p><p>Assessment must also screen for co-occurring apraxia of speech and dysarthria, since motor speech disorders frequently accompany aphasia after stroke and require differentiated treatment planning.</p>']],
