        [
            'title' => 'Evidence-Based Treatment Approaches',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Melodic Intonation Therapy (MIT) uses the preserved melodic/prosodic processing of the right hemisphere to facilitate verbal production in clients with severe nonfluent aphasia, intoning common phrases to a simple melodic contour and gradually fading the melody toward normal speech. It is best suited to clients with good auditory comprehension but severely limited verbal output.</p><p>Constraint-Induced Language Therapy (CILT), adapted from constraint-induced movement therapy, restricts compensatory strategies such as gesture or writing and requires verbal communication within intensive, massed-practice sessions, often using a barrier card game format, to drive use-dependent neuroplasticity.</p><p>Semantic Feature Analysis targets word-finding difficulty by guiding clients to generate semantic features of a target word (category, use, location, association) rather than the word itself, strengthening semantic network activation and supporting generalization to untrained words. Treatment selection should match the client\'s specific profile, severity, and personally meaningful communication goals.</p>']],
                ['type' => 'research_paper', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A 58-year-old right-handed man presents eight months post left MCA stroke with severe nonfluent Broca\'s aphasia: effortful, telegraphic speech limited to single words, intact auditory comprehension for everyday conversation, and a strong desire to communicate with his grandchildren. His clinician considers Melodic Intonation Therapy given his preserved comprehension and severely limited verbal output, versus Constraint-Induced Language Therapy given his tendency to over-rely on gesture. The team settles on an initial 8-week block of MIT targeting personally relevant functional phrases, with a plan to transition toward CILT for conversational generalization once single-word verbal output improves.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
