        [
            'title' => 'Living with Aphasia: Participation and Support',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>The Life Participation Approach to Aphasia (LPAA) reframes rehabilitation goals around what matters most to the person with aphasia and their communication partners, rather than exclusively targeting impairment-level language recovery. This includes training communication partners in supported conversation techniques, such as confirming understanding, using total communication (gesture, writing, drawing), and verifying message accuracy.</p><p>Aphasia significantly increases risk of social isolation and depression, since communication underlies most social participation; group therapy formats, aphasia cafes, and conversation groups provide low-pressure practice environments and peer support that individual impairment-focused therapy cannot replicate.</p><p>Clinicians should also address long-term supports, including vocational re-entry planning where appropriate, caregiver education, and connection to aphasia-specific community resources, recognizing that recovery and adjustment continue well beyond the acute and subacute rehabilitation phases.</p>']],
